/* Shortcut Press — JavaScript Fundamentals for the Browser guide content (generated by tools/site/build.mjs). */
window.SP_GUIDE = {"id":"javascript-basics","slug":"javascript-basics","title":"JavaScript Fundamentals for the Browser","software":"A code editor and a web browser","apps":["VS Code","Browser"],"intro":"Learn JavaScript by building a small expense tracker that runs in your browser: variables and types, functions and decisions, arrays and objects, changing the page with the DOM, handling forms and clicks, and loading and saving data. Every output in the guide comes from the practice data.","platformNote":"Shortcuts are shown for VS Code and Chrome or Edge on Windows and Linux. On a Mac, use Cmd in place of Ctrl and Option in place of Alt (the console shortcut is Cmd+Option+J).","files":[{"name":"index.html","title":"Expense tracker page","desc":"The page for the project: a form, a filter, a list and a total, with a little CSS built in.","size":"2 KB"},{"name":"app.js","title":"Starter script","desc":"An almost-empty script connected to the page, with a heading comment for each lesson.","size":"1 KB"},{"name":"expenses.json","title":"Sample expenses","desc":"Six expenses as JSON, loaded by the page in Lesson 6.","size":"1 KB"},{"name":"finished-app.js","title":"Finished script","desc":"The completed tracker, to compare with your own. Rename it app.js to try it.","size":"2 KB"}],"shortcuts":[{"keys":"ArrowUp","action":"Bring back the previous line in the console","app":"Browser"},{"keys":"Shift+Enter","action":"New line in the console without running","app":"Browser"},{"keys":"Ctrl+S","action":"Save the file","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Ctrl+/","action":"Comment or uncomment","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Space","action":"Show suggestions","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Ctrl+D","action":"Select the next match","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Alt+ArrowUp","action":"Move the line up","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Alt+ArrowDown","action":"Move the line down","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Shift+Alt+F","action":"Format the document","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Shift+P","action":"Command palette","app":"VS Code"},{"keys":"Ctrl+Shift+J","action":"Open the browser console","app":"Browser","practice":false,"note":"Your browser reserves this shortcut."},{"keys":"F12","action":"Open the developer tools","app":"Browser","practice":false,"note":"Your browser reserves this key."}],"lessons":[{"id":"basics","title":"Running JavaScript, variables and types","summary":"Try JavaScript in the browser console, connect a script to a page, store values in variables, and meet the basic types.","diagram":{"type":"terminal","shell":"console","lines":[[">","const fee = 45"],[">","typeof fee"],["","'number'"],[">","\"5\" + 3"],["","'53'"]],"highlight":[3]},"steps":[{"title":"The console","body":"<p>Every browser has a JavaScript console. Press <kbd>F12</kbd> (or <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> <kbd>Shift</kbd> <kbd>J</kbd> in Chrome and Edge) and choose <em>Console</em>. Type <code>2 + 3</code> and press Enter: 5. The console is a scratchpad for trying code; <kbd>ArrowUp</kbd> brings back what you typed before.</p>"},{"title":"Connect a script","body":"<p>Put the practice files in one folder. index.html already contains <code>&lt;script src=\"app.js\" defer&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code>; <code>defer</code> runs the script after the page has loaded. Open index.html in your browser with the console open: “app.js is connected” appears, printed by <code>console.log</code>.</p>"},{"title":"const and let","body":"<p><code>const fee = 45;</code> creates a variable that can't be reassigned; <code>let count = 0;</code> one that can (<code>count = count + 1;</code>). Use <code>const</code> by default and <code>let</code> when the value must change. (You'll see <code>var</code> in older code; it behaves differently and is best avoided.)</p>"},{"title":"Types and their surprises","body":"<p>Values have types: strings (<code>\"Bus pass\"</code>), numbers (<code>45</code>, <code>18.5</code>), booleans (<code>true</code>), plus <code>null</code> and <code>undefined</code>. <code>typeof</code> tells you which. Watch out: <code>\"5\" + 3</code> gives <code>\"53\"</code> because + joins text, while <code>\"5\" * 3</code> gives 15. And <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> is 0.30000000000000004, so format money with <code>.toFixed(2)</code>.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does \"5\" + 3 evaluate to?","options":["8","\"53\"","15","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"If either side of + is a string, JavaScript joins them as text."},{"q":"When should you use let instead of const?","options":["Always","When the variable will be reassigned","For numbers only","Inside functions only"],"answer":1,"explain":"const can't be reassigned; let can."}],"exercise":{"title":"Variables in the script","task":"<p>Under “Lesson 1” in app.js, create a const for a label (“Bus pass”), a const for an amount (45), and a let for a running total starting at 0. Add the amount to the total and log a sentence like “Bus pass: 45.00 (total 45.00)” with a template string.</p>","files":["index.html","app.js"],"solution":["<code>const label = \"Bus pass\";</code> and <code>const amount = 45;</code>","<code>let runningTotal = 0;</code> then <code>runningTotal = runningTotal + amount;</code>","<code>console.log(`${label}: ${amount.toFixed(2)} (total ${runningTotal.toFixed(2)})`);</code> (backticks make a template string).","Save, reload the page: the console shows <strong>Bus pass: 45.00 (total 45.00)</strong>."]}},{"id":"functions","title":"Functions and decisions","summary":"Write functions that take inputs and return results, use arrow functions, and make decisions with if, comparisons and logical operators.","diagram":{"type":"code","lang":"app.js","lines":["function formatMoney(amount) {","  return amount.toFixed(2);","}","const isBig = (amount) => amount >= 50;"],"highlight":[1,3],"notes":[[1,"Sends the result back"],[3,"Arrow function"]]},"steps":[{"title":"Functions","body":"<p>A function packages code to reuse: <code>function formatMoney(amount) { return amount.toFixed(2); }</code>. Call it with <code>formatMoney(18.5)</code>, which returns <code>\"18.50\"</code>. <code>amount</code> is a parameter: a name for whatever value is passed in.</p>"},{"title":"Arrow functions","body":"<p>A shorter form, common for small functions: <code>const isBig = (amount) =&gt; amount &gt;= 50;</code>. With a single expression after the arrow, its value is returned automatically. <code>isBig(64.2)</code> is <code>true</code>.</p>"},{"title":"if, else and comparisons","body":"<p><code>if (amount &gt;= 50) { … } else { … }</code>. Compare with <code>===</code> (equal value and type) and <code>!==</code>. Avoid <code>==</code>, which converts types first: <code>5 == \"5\"</code> is true but <code>5 === \"5\"</code> is false.</p>"},{"title":"Combining conditions","body":"<p><code>&amp;&amp;</code> means and, <code>||</code> or, <code>!</code> not. To check a label isn't empty and the amount is above zero: <code>if (label !== \"\" &amp;&amp; amount &gt; 0)</code>. You'll use exactly this check for the form in Lesson 5.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does formatMoney(18.5) return?","options":["18.5","\"18.50\"","18.50 (a number)","undefined"],"answer":1,"explain":"toFixed returns a string with two decimals."},{"q":"What is 5 === \"5\"?","options":["true","false","An error","undefined"],"answer":1,"explain":"=== also compares types: a number isn't a string."}],"exercise":{"title":"Two helper functions","task":"<p>Under “Lesson 2”, write <code>formatMoney(amount)</code> returning two decimals, and an arrow function <code>describe(label, amount)</code> that returns “Groceries: 64.20 (big)” when the amount is 50 or more and “Cinema: 24.00” otherwise. Log both examples.</p>","files":["app.js"],"solution":["<code>function formatMoney(amount) { return amount.toFixed(2); }</code>","<code>const describe = (label, amount) =&gt; { const text = `${label}: ${formatMoney(amount)}`; if (amount &gt;= 50) return text + \" (big)\"; return text; };</code>","<code>console.log(describe(\"Groceries\", 64.2));</code> → Groceries: 64.20 (big)","<code>console.log(describe(\"Cinema\", 24));</code> → Cinema: 24.00"]}},{"id":"data","title":"Arrays and objects","summary":"Store many values in arrays and describe things with objects, loop through them, and transform them with map, filter and reduce.","diagram":{"type":"table","head":["method","returns","example result"],"rows":[["filter","matching items","3 Food expenses"],["map","one value per item","labels in capitals"],["reduce","one combined value","total 271.60"]],"highlight":{"rows":[2]},"caption":"The three array methods you'll use most"},"steps":[{"title":"Objects","body":"<p>An object groups named values: <code>{ id: 2, label: \"Bus pass\", amount: 45, category: \"Transport\" }</code>. Read a property with a dot: <code>expense.amount</code>. Objects are how JavaScript describes things.</p>"},{"title":"Arrays of objects","body":"<p>An array is an ordered list: <code>const expenses = [ {…}, {…} ];</code>. <code>expenses.length</code> counts the items, <code>expenses[0]</code> is the first, and <code>expenses.push(newExpense)</code> adds one to the end. <code>for (const e of expenses) { … }</code> loops through them.</p>"},{"title":"filter and map","body":"<p><code>expenses.filter((e) =&gt; e.category === \"Food\")</code> returns a new array of the matching items: 3 of the 6 sample expenses. <code>expenses.map((e) =&gt; e.label.toUpperCase())</code> returns a new array with one result per item. Neither changes the original array.</p>"},{"title":"reduce","body":"<p><code>reduce</code> combines an array into one value. A total: <code>expenses.reduce((sum, e) =&gt; sum + e.amount, 0)</code>. It starts at 0 and adds each amount: <strong>271.6</strong> for the sample data. Food alone totals 141.45.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does filter return?","options":["A single item","A new array of items that pass the test","The original array, changed","true or false"],"answer":1,"explain":"filter keeps the items for which the function returns true, in a new array."},{"q":"What does expenses.reduce((sum, e) => sum + e.amount, 0) give for the sample data?","options":["6","271.6","141.45","64.2"],"answer":1,"explain":"The six amounts added together."}],"exercise":{"title":"Summarize the sample expenses","task":"<p>Paste the contents of <strong>expenses.json</strong> into app.js as <code>const sample = [ … ];</code>. Log the number of expenses, the labels of those of 50 or more, the total, and the Food total.</p>","files":["expenses.json","app.js"],"solution":["<code>console.log(sample.length);</code> → 6","<code>console.log(sample.filter((e) =&gt; e.amount &gt;= 50).map((e) =&gt; e.label));</code> → [\"Groceries\", \"Electricity\", \"Groceries\"]","<code>const total = sample.reduce((sum, e) =&gt; sum + e.amount, 0);</code> and <code>console.log(total.toFixed(2));</code> → <strong>271.60</strong>","<code>sample.filter((e) =&gt; e.category === \"Food\").reduce((s, e) =&gt; s + e.amount, 0).toFixed(2)</code> → <strong>141.45</strong>"]}},{"id":"dom","title":"Changing the page with the DOM","summary":"Find elements with querySelector, change their text and classes, and build a list from data with createElement.","diagram":{"type":"tree","root":"document","items":[["body",1],["ul#list",2,true],["li  Groceries (Food)  64.20",3],["li  Bus pass (Transport)  45.00",3],["p.total > strong#total",2]],"caption":"The DOM: the page as a tree of objects"},"steps":[{"title":"The DOM","body":"<p>When the browser loads HTML, it builds the <strong>Document Object Model</strong>: a tree of objects, one per element, that JavaScript can read and change. Change the DOM and the page updates instantly.</p>"},{"title":"Find elements","body":"<p><code>document.querySelector(\"#total\")</code> finds the first element matching a CSS selector, here the element with id total. <code>querySelectorAll(\".big\")</code> finds all matches. Store results in constants: <code>const list = document.querySelector(\"#list\");</code>.</p>"},{"title":"Change text and classes","body":"<p><code>element.textContent = \"271.60\"</code> replaces an element's text. <code>element.classList.add(\"big\")</code> adds a class (the page's CSS makes <code>li.big</code> bold); <code>remove</code> and <code>toggle</code> do what they say. Prefer <code>textContent</code> over <code>innerHTML</code> for text that comes from users, so it can never be run as HTML.</p>"},{"title":"Build elements","body":"<p><code>const li = document.createElement(\"li\");</code> makes an element, <code>li.textContent = …</code> fills it, and <code>list.append(li)</code> adds it to the page. Loop over the expenses to make one <code>li</code> per expense. Clearing the list first (<code>list.innerHTML = \"\"</code>) lets you redraw it whenever the data changes.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does document.querySelector(\"#list\") return?","options":["All lists","The first element with id list","The text of the list","A new list"],"answer":1,"explain":"querySelector returns the first element matching the selector."},{"q":"Why prefer textContent to innerHTML for user-entered text?","options":["It's faster to type","Text is never interpreted as HTML, so it can't inject markup or scripts","innerHTML doesn't work on lists","textContent adds styling"],"answer":1,"explain":"textContent treats everything as plain text."}],"exercise":{"title":"Render the list","task":"<p>Write a <code>render()</code> function that empties #list, adds an li for each expense in <code>sample</code> showing “label (category)” and the amount in a span, adds the class big to amounts of 50 or more, and puts the total in #total. Call it.</p>","files":["index.html","finished-app.js"],"solution":["<code>function render() { const list = document.querySelector(\"#list\"); list.innerHTML = \"\";</code>","<code>for (const e of sample) { const li = document.createElement(\"li\"); li.textContent = e.label + \" (\" + e.category + \")\";</code>","<code>const span = document.createElement(\"span\"); span.textContent = formatMoney(e.amount); li.append(span); if (e.amount &gt;= 50) li.classList.add(\"big\"); list.append(li); }</code>","<code>document.querySelector(\"#total\").textContent = formatMoney(sample.reduce((s, e) =&gt; s + e.amount, 0)); }</code> then <code>render();</code>","Reload: six rows, three in bold, and Total: <strong>271.60</strong>."]}},{"id":"events","title":"Events and forms","summary":"Respond to clicks, form submissions and changes, read and check form values, and handle many buttons with one listener.","diagram":{"type":"flow","nodes":["Submit form","preventDefault","Read and check values","Update data","render()"],"highlight":1,"title":"Handling a form without reloading the page"},"steps":[{"title":"addEventListener","body":"<p><code>element.addEventListener(\"click\", handler)</code> runs a function whenever the event happens. The handler receives an <code>event</code> object describing it; <code>event.target</code> is the element involved.</p>"},{"title":"Forms and preventDefault","body":"<p>Listen for <code>submit</code> on the form, not click on the button, so pressing Enter works too. A form's default action reloads the page; <code>event.preventDefault()</code> stops that so your code can handle it. Read values with <code>document.querySelector(\"#label\").value</code>.</p>"},{"title":"Values are text","body":"<p>Input values are always strings, even in a number field. Convert with <code>Number(...)</code>; an empty or invalid entry gives 0 or <code>NaN</code>. Check before adding: <code>if (!label || !(amount &gt; 0))</code>, show a message in the page's error paragraph, and <code>return</code> early.</p>"},{"title":"One listener for many buttons","body":"<p>Each list row gets a Delete button with <code>data-id</code> set to the expense's id. Instead of a listener per button, put one on the list: in the handler, check <code>event.target.matches(\"button\")</code>, read <code>event.target.dataset.id</code>, and filter that expense out. This <em>event delegation</em> also works for rows added later.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why listen for submit on the form instead of click on the button?","options":["click doesn't work on buttons","submit also fires when the user presses Enter in a field","It's faster","preventDefault only works with submit"],"answer":1,"explain":"The form's submit event covers every way of submitting it."},{"q":"What does Number(\"\") return?","options":["NaN","0","undefined","An error"],"answer":1,"explain":"An empty string converts to 0, so check amount > 0."}],"exercise":{"title":"Add, check and delete","task":"<p>Make the form add an expense (with the chosen category) and redraw the list, show an error for an empty label or an amount of zero, and make each row's Delete button remove that expense. Switch your data to a <code>let expenses</code> array.</p>","files":["index.html","finished-app.js"],"solution":["<code>let expenses = [...sample];</code> and change render to use <code>expenses</code>.","Submit handler: <code>event.preventDefault();</code> read label (trimmed), amount (<code>Number</code>) and category; if invalid, set the error text, unhide it, and return.","Otherwise <code>expenses.push({ id: Date.now(), label, amount, category }); render(); event.target.reset();</code>","In render, add a <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> with <code>del.dataset.id = e.id</code> to each row.","On #list: <code>if (!event.target.matches(\"button\")) return; const id = Number(event.target.dataset.id); expenses = expenses.filter((e) =&gt; e.id !== id); render();</code>"]}},{"id":"fetch-storage","title":"Loading and saving data","summary":"Load JSON with fetch and async/await from a local server, save the user's data in localStorage, and filter what's shown.","diagram":{"type":"flow","nodes":["Page loads","Saved data?","Yes: localStorage","No: fetch expenses.json","render()"],"highlight":1,"title":"Where the expenses come from"},"steps":[{"title":"Run a local server","body":"<p>Browsers block <code>fetch</code> on pages opened straight from a file. In VS Code, install the <em>Live Server</em> extension, right-click index.html and choose <em>Open with Live Server</em>: the page opens from a local address such as <code>http://127.0.0.1:5500</code>. (Or run <code>python -m http.server</code> in the folder.)</p>"},{"title":"fetch with async/await","body":"<p><code>async function load() { const response = await fetch(\"expenses.json\"); expenses = await response.json(); render(); }</code>. <code>await</code> waits for the file to arrive without freezing the page. Wrap it in <code>try { … } catch (err) { … }</code> to handle a missing file.</p>"},{"title":"localStorage","body":"<p><code>localStorage.setItem(\"pocket-expenses\", JSON.stringify(expenses))</code> saves the array as text in the browser; <code>JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(\"pocket-expenses\"))</code> reads it back. Call your save function after every change. Data stays in that browser on that device only.</p>"},{"title":"Filter the view","body":"<p>Listen for <code>change</code> on the #filter select and call <code>render</code>. Inside render, show <code>expenses</code> or <code>expenses.filter((e) =&gt; e.category === filter)</code> and total only what's shown. Choosing Food with the sample data shows 3 rows totaling 141.45.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"Why does fetch(\"expenses.json\") fail when index.html is opened as a file?","options":["JSON isn't allowed in browsers","Browsers block fetch requests from file:// pages; a local server fixes it","fetch needs a full URL","The file is too big"],"answer":1,"explain":"Serving the folder over http lets the page fetch its own files."},{"q":"Why JSON.stringify before localStorage.setItem?","options":["To compress the data","localStorage only stores strings","To encrypt it","It's optional"],"answer":1,"explain":"Objects must be turned into text, and parsed back when read."}],"exercise":{"title":"Load, save and filter","task":"<p>Serve the folder with Live Server. Write <code>load()</code> to use saved data if there is any, otherwise fetch expenses.json; save after every add or delete; and make the filter work. Add an expense, reload the page, and check it's still there.</p>","files":["expenses.json","finished-app.js"],"solution":["Right-click index.html › <em>Open with Live Server</em>.","<code>function save() { localStorage.setItem(\"pocket-expenses\", JSON.stringify(expenses)); }</code>; call it after push and after filter-out.","<code>async function load() { const saved = localStorage.getItem(\"pocket-expenses\"); if (saved) { expenses = JSON.parse(saved); } else { const response = await fetch(\"expenses.json\"); expenses = await response.json(); } render(); }</code> and call <code>load()</code>.","Filter: <code>document.querySelector(\"#filter\").addEventListener(\"change\", render);</code> and filter inside render. Food: 3 rows, total 141.45.","Add an expense and reload: it's still listed. Compare with finished-app.js."]}}],"extras":{"project":{"id":"bonus-chart","title":"Bonus project: a category chart without libraries","summary":"Total spending by category with reduce, and draw a simple bar chart with plain elements and CSS widths that updates whenever the data changes.","diagram":{"type":"bars","values":[141.45,61.15,45,24],"labels":["Food","Bills","Transport","Fun"],"highlight":0,"showValues":true,"title":"Spending by category in the sample data"},"steps":[{"title":"Totals by category","body":"<p>Reduce into an object: <code>const byCategory = expenses.reduce((acc, e) =&gt; { acc[e.category] = (acc[e.category] || 0) + e.amount; return acc; }, {});</code>. For the sample data: Food 141.45, Bills 61.15, Transport 45, Fun 24.</p>"},{"title":"Bars from divs","body":"<p>For each category, create a row with a label and a div whose width is a percentage of the largest total: <code>bar.style.width = (value / max * 100) + \"%\";</code>. Sort first with <code>Object.entries(byCategory).sort((a, b) =&gt; b[1] - a[1])</code>.</p>"},{"title":"Keep it in sync","body":"<p>Call your <code>drawChart()</code> at the end of <code>render()</code>, so every add, delete and filter redraws it. Give the chart a text alternative, such as an <code>aria-label</code> on its container listing the totals.</p>"}],"quiz":[{"q":"What does Object.entries({Food: 141.45, Fun: 24}) return?","options":["[\"Food\", \"Fun\"]","[[\"Food\", 141.45], [\"Fun\", 24]]","{Food, Fun}","165.45"],"answer":1,"explain":"An array of [key, value] pairs, which you can sort."},{"q":"Food is the largest total (141.45). How wide is the Bills bar (61.15)?","options":["61.15%","About 43%","50%","100%"],"answer":1,"explain":"61.15 ÷ 141.45 ≈ 0.432, so about 43%."}],"exercise":{"title":"Draw the chart","task":"<p>Add a chart section to index.html and a <code>drawChart()</code> function that shows a bar per category, widest for the largest total, redrawn on every change.</p>","files":["index.html","finished-app.js"],"solution":["In index.html: <code>&lt;section id=\"chart\" aria-label=\"Spending by category\"&gt;&lt;/section&gt;</code>.","In drawChart: compute <code>byCategory</code>, sort entries, find <code>max</code>, clear the section.","For each entry: a div row with a label span and a bar div with <code>style.width</code> as a percentage and a background color.","Call drawChart() at the end of render(). Bars: Food 100%, Bills about 43%, Transport about 32%, Fun about 17%."]}},"quizbank":[{"q":"Which keyword declares a variable that can't be reassigned?","options":["let","const","var","static"],"answer":1,"explain":"const bindings can't be reassigned."},{"q":"What does typeof \"42\" return?","options":["\"number\"","\"string\"","\"text\"","42"],"answer":1,"explain":"Quoted values are strings."},{"q":"What does [3, 8, 12].map((n) => n * 2) return?","options":["[3, 8, 12]","[6, 16, 24]","46","[12]"],"answer":1,"explain":"map returns a new array with each item transformed."},{"q":"What does event.preventDefault() do in a submit handler?","options":["Deletes the form","Stops the browser's default action of reloading the page","Clears the inputs","Validates the form"],"answer":1,"explain":"Your code handles the submission instead."},{"q":"What does await do inside an async function?","options":["Freezes the page until done","Pauses that function until a promise finishes, without blocking the page","Repeats the line","Throws an error"],"answer":1,"explain":"Other code and the page stay responsive."},{"q":"Where does localStorage keep data?","options":["On a server","In the browser, on that device","In the HTML file","In the cloud"],"answer":1,"explain":"It's per browser and per site."},{"q":"Which method finds all elements with the class big?","options":["querySelector(\".big\")","querySelectorAll(\".big\")","getElement(\"big\")","find(\".big\")"],"answer":1,"explain":"querySelectorAll returns every match."},{"q":"What does (0.1 + 0.2).toFixed(2) give?","options":["0.3","\"0.30\"","0.30000000000000004","\"0.3\""],"answer":1,"explain":"toFixed rounds and returns a string with two decimals."}]},"versionNote":"Software makers update their programs, so menus and button locations can change between versions. This guide is revised when an update changes the steps, and the revised version appears the next time you open it."};
